<http://digistrucmed.org/ClassOfRecommendation_2a> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2a" .
<http://digistrucmed.org/semantic-unit/lu_0074e29f31a1f0feb082e0064bee46f60bf6b5ae3f8703d1231341374ed97e52_n0006> <http://digistrucmed.org/hasTextUnit> <http://digistrucmed.org/text-unit/lu_0074e29f31a1f0feb082e0064bee46f60bf6b5ae3f8703d1231341374ed97e52_n0006/tu_0008> .
<http://digistrucmed.org/normative-assertion/lu_0074e29f31a1f0feb082e0064bee46f60bf6b5ae3f8703d1231341374ed97e52_n0006/na_0011> <http://digistrucmed.org/deonticForce> "PERMISSIVE" .
<http://digistrucmed.org/text-unit/lu_0074e29f31a1f0feb082e0064bee46f60bf6b5ae3f8703d1231341374ed97e52_n0006/tu_0013> <http://digistrucmed.org/verbatimText> "• HIIT may then be applied to selected low-risk stable patients. A 10 min warm-up phase ( <VT_1 ) is followed by high-intensity intervals ( >VT_2 , RPE \\geq 15 ), interrupted by recovery intervals ( <VT_1 ) (times of intervals according to the protocol, i.e. 4 \\times 4 min or less)" .
